package standard.io

import standard.wasi.ComponentStreamWrite
import standard.wasi.EndingLedger
import standard.wasi.EventPayload
import standard.wasi.OpenFlag
import standard.wasi.Waitable
import standard.wasi.noteCopyProgress
import standard.wasi.CopyResult
import standard.wasi.IoError
import standard.text.Charset

/// Async, Resource-aware output stream over a byte transport. `WasiOutputStream`
/// drives a p3 `stream<u8>` write; other subclasses (a TLS stream, say) encrypt
/// bytes before pushing them to an underlying stream. The fs, socket, stdout and
/// TLS libraries hand this out.
///
/// THE CONTRACT, in the same order as `AsyncInputStream`'s, which states the
/// shared rules in full. Only the differences are written out here, and each is a
/// difference in the transport rather than in taste.
///
/// (1) THERE IS NO END OF STREAM TO REPORT, so the rule that empty means EOF has
///     no twin here. A write is accepted or it fails; there is no third answer
///     and no short write — `write` returns `Unit`, so a partial one has nowhere
///     to be reported and is retried until the whole buffer is gone.
///
/// (2) NO OPERATION ANSWERS A STREAM THAT CANNOT SEND WITH CLEAN SUCCESS,
///     including the empty write, through the same `checkAlive` hook. Two
///     differences from the read side, both real. A FINISHED write stream FAILS
///     where a finished read stream answers empty, because a stream that has
///     ended can never accept another byte while an ended read stream has a
///     legitimate answer to give. And there is no (3) to defer to, so nothing
///     here goes to the transport before asking.
///
///     `flush` and `close` are not these operations. They ask what became of what
///     was already sent rather than whether more could be, they answer from
///     `recordedFailure`, and a stream that ended cleanly answers `Ok(())`.
///
/// (3) A PAYLOAD TRANSFERRED IS A PAYLOAD DELIVERED reads differently here but is
///     not absent. The bytes a partial copy accepted are already the transport's
///     and the caller only learns whether ALL of them got there, so there is no
///     half-answer to hand back. What survives is that the count on a copy is
///     believed whatever else that copy reported: a `Dropped` copy that took the
///     last of the buffer wrote the buffer, and a cancelled one that took half of
///     it left a hole the stream has to be fenced against.
///
/// (4) CANCELLED IS THIS OPERATION, not the transport — identical to the read
///     side. It fails with the stream's `canceledError` and records NO ending,
///     because the runtime reads the one-shot result future only when a copy
///     reports the stream `Dropped`; a cancel recorded as the ending would make
///     the wrapper skip the read a late host error is waiting in.
///
///     A cancel that transferred nothing also leaves the stream OPEN, which is
///     the read side's rule verbatim. A write that got PART of a buffer out does
///     not: the byte stream now has a hole that no later write could see, so the
///     stream is fenced as `Torn` — unusable, but not ended, and its result
///     future still owed a read.
///
///     THE HOLE IS NOT MADE BY ANY ONE COPY, which is why the fence is not in an
///     arm. A write of n bytes is a sequence of copies, and the buffer is left half
///     out whenever that sequence stops early — most often at an INTERRUPT, which
///     reaches no arm at all, because the fiber is failed where it parked. So the
///     fence is on the way out of `writeBytes`, where every abnormal exit passes,
///     and the arms only decide what to report. See `fenceIncomplete`.
///
/// (5)/(6) NO LENGTHS TO GET WRONG: a write is handed a buffer rather than a
///     count, so there is no negative length to reject and nothing to split into
///     families — it stops when the buffer is gone. The read side's (6) says why
///     the mirror image (a read that stops on a count the caller named) is not
///     that class's business either.
///
/// (7) THE EXTENSION POINTS ARE `protected` AND THE PUBLIC API IS `final`, as on
///     the read side.
///
/// (8) WHAT A SUBCLASS OWES THE BASE: that `writeBytes` writes ALL of the bytes
///     it is handed or fails, and that `checkAlive` and `recordedFailure` answer
///     honestly.
public abstract class AsyncOutputStream<E>
    where E: IoError =
    /// Write all bytes, honoring whatever backpressure protocol the transport
    /// requires.
    ///
    /// An EMPTY buffer is not a no-op: nothing is written, but the stream is still
    /// asked whether it could have taken bytes, so a dead or finished stream
    /// reports itself instead of answering with the clean `Ok(())` that a caller
    /// reads as "written". This is the write-side twin of `read(0)`, and the
    /// health check comes FIRST here where it comes second there — there, a dead
    /// transport may still be holding bytes that are the caller's; here, a dead
    /// transport has nothing to hand back.
    ///
    /// It is not a BARRIER. `flush` is the operation that means "wait for what is
    /// already posted" and the only one that promises it; a zero-length write
    /// does no transport work at all, so that a caller who wants to know whether
    /// the stream is alive need not pay a network round trip to find out.
    public final async function write(self, bytes: ReadonlySlice<Uint8>): Async<Unit, E> =
        await self.checkAlive()
        if bytes.length == 0 then () else await self.writeBytes(bytes)

    /// Write every byte of a non-empty view, or fail. The health check above has
    /// already run, but it does NOT stand in for re-checking between copies: this
    /// method awaits, and a sibling fiber can close the stream while it is parked.
    protected abstract function writeBytes(self, bytes: ReadonlySlice<Uint8>): Async<Unit, E>

    /// Fail with the transport failure this stream recorded, or with the reason it
    /// can no longer accept bytes.
    ///
    /// Abstract for the reason its read twin is: the only available default is
    /// "nothing ever goes wrong here", which is false for every transport that can
    /// fail, and a default is an answer given on a subclass's behalf.
    protected abstract function checkAlive(self): Async<Unit, E>

    /// Wait until every write already issued on this stream has been accepted by
    /// the transport, then report any failure the stream has recorded.
    ///
    /// The contract stops at *accepted*, deliberately. Under a completion model a
    /// `write` resolves only once the host has taken its bytes, so there is no
    /// buffer here to push; what flush adds is the wait for a write still in
    /// flight — a concurrent writer's, or one this fiber posted and did not await.
    /// It does NOT promise the bytes reached the disk or the peer, and it cannot:
    /// the only report of a late failure is the transport's one-shot result
    /// future, which resolves once the write end is gone. `close` is the
    /// durability point — it ends the stream and then reads that future.
    ///
    /// The wait comes first and the report second, which is the opposite order
    /// from every other operation: what flush reports is the outcome of work that
    /// was already posted when it was called, so reporting first would answer from
    /// before the very writes it exists to wait for.
    public final async function flush(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        await self.flushPending()
        await Async.fromResult(self.recordedFailure())

    /// Wait for the writes already in flight on this stream. Reporting is the
    /// base's job — see `flush`.
    protected abstract function flushPending(self): Async<Unit, E>

    /// What became of what was already written: the failure this stream has
    /// RECORDED, and nothing about whether it could take more.
    ///
    /// The question `flush` and `close` both ask, which is why it is here and
    /// abstract rather than each subclass's private business — they must answer it
    /// alike, or a stream that ended cleanly with every byte delivered tells one
    /// of them it is fine and the other that it is closed.
    ///
    /// Distinct from `checkAlive`, which answers "could I send more" and must fail
    /// on a finished stream (2). Nothing that only reports on bytes already
    /// accepted goes through that hook.
    protected abstract function recordedFailure(self): Result<Unit, E>

    /// End this stream now and report what the transport made of it.
    ///
    /// Optional, and symmetric with `AsyncInputStream.close`. The `Resource` that
    /// handed this stream out ENDS it on release whether or not this was called —
    /// its finalizer runs `dropHandles`, which drops the write end and reads the
    /// transport's verdict but CANCELS nothing and REPORTS nothing (a finalizer
    /// has nowhere to put an error, so it latches the verdict and leaves it). It
    /// can skip the cancel because by release time the scope has already unwound
    /// every fiber that could be mid-write: the guarantee that nothing is left in
    /// flight comes from that unwinding, not from the finalizer doing the
    /// cancelling. What calling `close` here adds is *when* (now, not at the end of
    /// the scope), *what came of it* (the verdict the finalizer would swallow), and
    /// the cancel of any write still in flight. Using the stream afterwards is
    /// undefined.
    ///
    /// On the write side "what came of it" is the whole point: a late host error —
    /// a disk that filled after the bytes were accepted, a connection reset after
    /// the last write — arrives only on the transport's one-shot result future,
    /// and that future resolves only once the write end is gone. So nothing short
    /// of ending the stream can observe it, and a finalizer, which cannot fail,
    /// has nowhere to report it. Call this when that error matters.
    ///
    /// A write in flight on this stream, in this fiber or another, is CANCELLED
    /// rather than waited for: a write blocked on a peer that never drains would
    /// never complete, so there is no in-flight operation that closing could
    /// promise to outlive. Those fibers fail with the origin's `Canceled`.
    public abstract function close(self): Async<Unit, E>

    /// UTF-8-encode the string and write all its bytes. v1 only supports UTF-8.
    public final async function writeString(self, string: String, charset: Charset):
        Async<Unit, E> =
        match charset with
            case Charset.Utf8 => await self.write(string.bytes().readonly)
            case _ => panic "unsupported charset"

/// What a stream owes the byte stream when a write stops with part of its buffer
/// already on the transport.
///
/// A buffer is one thing to the caller and a prefix to the transport, so a write
/// that stops half way leaves a HOLE nothing downstream can see. The question
/// this answers is not who owns the stream or how long it lives — that was the
/// old answer and it is the wrong question. It is whether a LATER write can be
/// told apart from a continuation of the torn one. Where it cannot, the damage is
/// silent, and a stream that goes on accepting writes goes on quietly producing
/// output that reads as correct.
public enum TornWrite =
    /// Retire the stream. Nothing more may be written to it and the write that
    /// tore it says why.
    ///
    /// For everything whose framing lives ABOVE the byte stream — a protocol on a
    /// socket, a record in a file — because nothing this layer could emit would
    /// mark the hole, and because the caller has somewhere to go: a stream handed
    /// out through a `Resource` is bounded by the scope that holds it, so losing
    /// one means reconnecting or reopening, which is a thing programs know how to
    /// do.
    Fence
    /// Keep the stream and make the hole VISIBLE instead: the next write is
    /// preceded by `separator`, so a consumer that frames on that byte sees a
    /// truncated record followed by a fresh one rather than the two spliced into
    /// each other.
    ///
    /// For the streams nothing can reopen — process stdio above all, which is made
    /// once, shared by every fiber and never released. Fencing one would let a
    /// single interrupted `writeLine` silence the component for the rest of its
    /// life; not fencing it, and doing nothing else, merges the next line onto the
    /// torn one and reports nothing to anybody. A visible loss is worth more than
    /// either: a component that cannot print is a component nobody can diagnose,
    /// and a log line that is obviously broken is one nobody misreads.
    Reframe(Uint8)

/// A p3-stream-backed output stream.
///
/// `sink` describes the whole host operation — which writable end, how to post a
/// copy, and the result future that says why the stream ended. The runtime drives
/// it. What is left here is what the stream's type cannot say: that a partial
/// write must be retried, that a vanished reader is a failure rather than an
/// ending, and which error to raise when the host recorded no reason.
///
/// INTERNAL, like its read twin: it owns host handles and is only handed out
/// attached to the `Resource` that releases them. See `resource`.
internal class WasiOutputStream<E>(
    public sink: ComponentStreamWrite<E>,
    // The bit behind `sink.stillOpen` — the record above was rebuilt with a
    // probe over this flag (`withStillOpen`, in `resource`). Flipped entering
    // `Closing` and entering `Released`: the two transitions a fiber other
    // than the write-lock holder can make, and the ones after which a posted
    // copy would chase a dropped handle. `Ended` and `Torn` are latched by the
    // lock holder itself, which never posts past them, and the handle is still
    // owned there. See `OpenFlag`.
    openFlag: OpenFlag,
    // Error to raise when the host drops the stream mid-write and the result
    // future reports no failure. Origin-specific (a connection-closed
    // `NetworkError`, an I/O `FileSystemError`). A dropped write can never make
    // progress, so it must fail rather than report zero bytes and spin.
    // Kept distinct from the stream's `canceledError` (on the `ComponentStreamWrite`):
    // this one says "the transport is gone", that one says "somebody closed this
    // under you", and only the caller can tell which of those it wanted to retry.
    public closedError: E,
    // Writes post only in `Open`. See `StreamState` — the write side is what its
    // `Ended`/`Closing` split is for: the result future is one-shot, and those two
    // states are precisely "the runtime already read it" and "nobody has, and
    // `close` is about to".
    mutable state: StreamState,
    mutable finishError: Option<E>,
    // What this stream owes the byte stream when a write leaves the buffer half
    // out. See `TornWrite` and `fenceIncomplete`; the answer is a property of what
    // reads the far end, so whoever builds the stream decides it.
    tornWrite: TornWrite,
    // Whether a tear left a separator owed to the next write. Only ever set under
    // `TornWrite.Reframe`.
    public mutable owedSeparator: Bool,
    // How much of the buffer the write in progress has got out. A field rather than
    // a local because the fence that reads it runs on the way OUT of the write,
    // including the way out an interrupt takes, where a local is gone.
    // Single-writer by construction: only the holder of `writeLock` touches it,
    // and it is reset when that lock is taken.
    public mutable written: Int32,
    // Held across a whole `write`, which the runtime's per-copy lock cannot do —
    // see `writeBytes`. Non-reentrant on purpose: `write` and `flush` are the only
    // holders and neither is reachable from inside the other, so an acquire that
    // finds this fiber already holding it is a bug in this file.
    writeLock: Mutex
) extends AsyncOutputStream<E>()
    where E: IoError =
    protected override async function writeBytes(self, bytes: ReadonlySlice<Uint8>):
        Async<Unit, E> =
        // The runtime serializes each individual copy on this stream end, because
        // p3 permits only one outstanding write per end. But a partial write is a
        // LOOP of copies, and a second fiber slipping between two of them would
        // interleave its bytes into the middle of this buffer. That is not an ABI
        // invariant — it is this stream's promise about what `write` means — so it
        // is ours to keep, with a mutex held across the whole retry loop.
        //
        // `orPanicWith`: the only way this acquire can fail is this fiber already
        // holding the lock, which nothing here does. An invariant of this class
        // rather than a condition a caller could recover from, so `MutexError`
        // stays out of `write`'s signature.
        let _held = use self
            .writeLock
            .acquire()
            .orPanicWith((_: MutexError) => "WasiOutputStream.write re-entered its own write lock")
        await self.writeReframed(bytes)

    /// Pay off the separator a torn write left owed, then write the caller's
    /// buffer. Only `TornWrite.Reframe` ever owes one — a fenced stream takes no
    /// further writes, so it has nobody to mark the hole for.
    ///
    /// The separator goes out as a write of its own rather than being spliced onto
    /// the front of `bytes`, which would copy the caller's whole buffer to prepend
    /// one byte. A ONE-BYTE write cannot itself tear: it either gets its byte out
    /// and succeeds, or gets nothing out, and nothing out is not a hole — so the
    /// debt survives to the next write instead of compounding.
    ///
    /// EACH PHASE FENCES ITSELF, against its own buffer. The tear accounting for
    /// a write is measured against that write and no other: one `fenceIncomplete`
    /// wrapped around both phases judged `written` — which still counted the
    /// separator's byte — against the CALLER's length, so an interrupt landing
    /// after the separator went out but before the debt was cleared re-armed a
    /// debt that was already paid, and the next console write opened with a
    /// phantom blank line. A debt is paid exactly when its byte went out
    /// (`written >= 1` — a raced cancel that took the byte counts it), and a
    /// paid debt stays paid on every exit path.
    ///
    /// The accounting runs inside the `foldCause` handlers, which the
    /// interpreter invokes in the same step that settles the inner write — so
    /// there is no boundary between learning the outcome and recording it that
    /// another fiber, or an interrupt, could land in.
    ///
    /// The handlers are named rather than written inline because a multi-line
    /// lambda argument swallows the comma that follows it — the layout rules
    /// put it inside the lambda's own block. `foldCause` and not `catchAll`:
    /// an interruption is not a typed failure, and it is the case this exists
    /// for. Every other abnormal exit passes through here too and needs
    /// nothing from it: a transport failure or a vanished reader has already
    /// recorded an ending, and `tear` only fences a stream that is still
    /// `Open`.
    async function writeReframed(self, bytes: ReadonlySlice<Uint8>): Async<Unit, E> =
        match self.tornWrite with
            case TornWrite.Reframe(separator) =>
                if self.owedSeparator then
                    self.written = 0i32
                    let paidOnCause: (Cause<E>) => Async<Unit, E> =
                        (cause: Cause<E>) =>
                            if self.written >= 1i32 then self.owedSeparator = false
                            Async<Unit, E>.fromCause(cause)
                    let paidOnWritten: (Unit) => Async<Unit, E> =
                        (_: Unit) =>
                            self.owedSeparator = false
                            Async.succeed(())
                    await self
                        .writeLocked([| separator |].readonly)
                        .foldCause<Unit, E>(paidOnCause, paidOnWritten)
            case TornWrite.Fence => ()
        self.written = 0i32
        let onCause: (Cause<E>) => Async<Unit, E> =
            (cause: Cause<E>) =>
                self.fenceIncomplete(bytes.length)
                Async<Unit, E>.fromCause(cause)
        let onWritten: (Unit) => Async<Unit, E> = (written: Unit) => Async.succeed(written)
        await self.writeLocked(bytes).foldCause<Unit, E>(onCause, onWritten)

    async function writeLocked(self, bytes: ReadonlySlice<Uint8>): Async<Unit, E> =
        let mutable remaining = bytes
        let mutable emptyCopies = 0
        let mutable posting = true
        let mutable stillOpen = true
        while posting do
            // DONE IS ASKED FIRST, before the stream is asked whether it is still
            // alive: the buffer is on the transport already, and what becomes of
            // the stream after that is the next operation's news. The other order
            // reports a write that delivered every byte as a failure whenever a
            // sibling closes the stream while the last copy is in flight.
            if remaining.isEmpty() then
                posting = false
            else
                // The state check is INSIDE the loop. A partial write is a
                // sequence of copies with a suspension between each pair, and a
                // sibling `close` landing in one of those windows drops the
                // handle — after which the host reuses the index and the next copy
                // goes to an unrelated stream, without failing.
                //
                // Validated-since-the-last-suspension is NOT the whole rule,
                // because this check and the post are separate interpreter
                // steps and the fairness budget can run the sibling `close`
                // between them. The last word is the sink's `stillOpen` probe —
                // a view of this object's own state, consulted by the runtime
                // INSIDE the copy-posting step — which fails the write with
                // `canceledError` exactly as the close would have, had it found
                // this fiber parked. This check stays for the common case: it
                // answers from the latch without a failure, and keeps the
                // probe's `false` arm the rarity it should be.
                match self.state with
                    case StreamState.Open =>
                        // Advancing the view does not copy the remaining bytes.
                        //
                        // No `catchAll`, which is the point of the triple: a
                        // failure out of this operation means one thing only —
                        // this fiber was detached from the handle by somebody's
                        // `close` — and a detached fiber has learnt nothing about
                        // the stream to record.
                        let copied = await Async<Unit, E>.streamWrite(self.sink, remaining)
                        // Counted before it is judged, and for every result. A
                        // cancelled or dropped copy still reports what the host
                        // took, and what the host took is the difference between a
                        // write that failed and a byte stream with a hole in it.
                        // On the cancel path the runtime hands this count over for
                        // that reason alone — see `settleCancelledRead`.
                        self
                            .written = self.written + copied._1
                        remaining = remaining [| copied._1.. |]
                        if copied._0 == CopyResult.Dropped then
                            // The stream ended under this write. This is the only
                            // arm the runtime consults the one-shot result future
                            // for, so it is the only place an ending is recorded.
                            //
                            // WHICH reason is three cases and they are not the
                            // same fact. The host's own is recorded whatever else
                            // happened: it is the truth about the stream and
                            // `close` exists to report it. With no reason given, a
                            // copy that left bytes behind is the origin's generic
                            // "the transport is gone" — and one that took
                            // everything left is not a failure at all. `write`
                            // promises the bytes were accepted and they were, so
                            // the ending is still recorded (nothing may post
                            // again, and the spent future must not be read twice)
                            // but with nothing in it: `close` answers `Ok` and the
                            // next WRITE reports the stream is over.
                            match copied._2 with
                                case Some(reason) =>
                                    self.latchEnding(Some(reason))
                                    if self.written < bytes.length then await Async.fail(reason)
                                    else posting = false
                                case None =>
                                    if self.written < bytes.length then
                                        self.latchEnding(Some(self.closedError))
                                        await Async.fail(self.closedError)
                                    else
                                        let noReason: Option<E> = None
                                        self.latchEnding(noReason)
                                        posting = false
                        else
                            if copied._0 == CopyResult.Cancelled then
                                // This WRITE was cancelled, which says nothing about
                                // the stream: somebody force-closed the handle under
                                // this fiber, or this fiber is being interrupted. So
                                // it reports the stream's `canceledError` rather than
                                // the origin's "the transport is gone", and records no
                                // ending (4).
                                //
                                // Whether it left a hole is the fence's question, asked
                                // on the way out of the write as a whole. Here there is
                                // only what to report, and a cancel that took the last
                                // of the buffer has nothing to report: the bytes are the
                                // transport's, so the loop goes round and stops on
                                // `done`.
                                if remaining.isEmpty() then ()
                                else await Async.fail(self.sink.canceledError)
                            else
                                // A completed copy that moved no bytes makes no
                                // progress, and this loop's exit condition is progress.
                                // The bound is shared with the library's other copy
                                // loops — see `emptyCopyLimit`.
                                emptyCopies =
                                    noteCopyProgress(
                                        emptyCopies,
                                        copied._1,
                                        "output stream: a host copy accepted no bytes and did not end the stream"
                                    )
                    case _ =>
                        stillOpen = false
                        posting = false
        if !stillOpen then await Async.fromResult(self.closedResult()) else ()

    /// Record that a copy observed the stream ending, and the transport's reason
    /// if it gave one. The result future is one-shot and the runtime has already
    /// consumed it by the time anything gets here, so every later write answers
    /// from the latch — and so does `close`, which is why the reason is recorded
    /// and not merely the ending. An ending with nothing in it reads as "no
    /// complaints".
    ///
    /// That the future has been spent is NOT recorded here, and used to be. It is
    /// a fact about a handle rather than about this stream, and recording it here
    /// meant recording it a fiber-step after the runtime did the reading — a gap
    /// an interrupt fits inside. It lives on the handle now; see `EndingLedger`.
    ///
    /// The STATE and the reason are recorded only from `Open`, which is what keeps
    /// `close` honest: a write that `close` cancelled fails with the stream's
    /// `canceledError` and would otherwise be recorded as how the stream ended, so
    /// `close` would report back the cancellation it caused instead of the late
    /// host error it exists to surface. The same guard stops an `Ended` being
    /// written over a close's `Released`, which would make `dropHandles` release
    /// host handles twice. And the first writer to discover an ending records it —
    /// a straggler is reporting a consequence, not the cause.
    function latchEnding(self, reason: Option<E>): Unit =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Open =>
                self.state = StreamState.Ended
                self.finishError = reason
            case _ => ()

    /// Fence a stream that has half of a buffer on the transport and no way to
    /// finish it. Not an ending: see `StreamState.Torn`, and `fenceIncomplete`
    /// for who asks.
    function tear(self, error: E): Unit =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Open =>
                self.state = StreamState.Torn
                self.finishError = Some(error)
            case _ => ()

    /// Fence the stream if the write that is unwinding left the buffer half out.
    ///
    /// Called on every failed exit from a write, which is the only place that
    /// covers every way of leaving one: an interrupt reaches no arm, and the arms
    /// cannot see how many copies came before them anyway.
    ///
    /// The two edges are the two halves of (4). NOTHING out is the ordinary
    /// cancelled write: no hole, and the stream stays open, exactly as a cancelled
    /// read leaves its stream readable. EVERYTHING out is not a failure at all as
    /// far as the byte stream is concerned — the buffer is whole on the transport —
    /// so it is not fenced either, even when the operation itself then failed.
    ///
    /// `canceledError` and not `closedError`: nothing has gone wrong with the
    /// transport, an operation on it was taken away mid-buffer, and only one of
    /// those means "reconnect".
    ///
    /// WHAT IS OWED for a hole is `TornWrite`'s question and not this one's, and
    /// it is answered by whoever built the stream, because it is a fact about what
    /// reads the far end rather than about the write. Here there is only whether a
    /// hole was made.
    public function fenceIncomplete(self, total: Int32): Unit =
        if self.written > 0i32 && self.written < total then
            match self.tornWrite with
                case TornWrite.Fence => self.tear(self.sink.canceledError)
                case TornWrite.Reframe(_) => self.owedSeparator = true
        else
            ()

    /// Why this stream can no longer take a byte, if it cannot. The single
    /// statement of that rule; `checkAlive` and `closedResult` are two spellings
    /// of it.
    ///
    /// A recorded failure — a transport error, a vanished reader, a torn write —
    /// is reported as itself. A stream somebody CLOSED reports `canceledError`
    /// rather than the origin's "the transport is gone", for the reason the read
    /// side reports it (invariant (9) there): that is the error the runtime raised
    /// in whichever fibers were on the handle when the close cancelled them, and a
    /// fiber arriving an instant later is owed the same answer rather than a
    /// differently-worded one chosen by the scheduler.
    public function unusable(self): Option<E> =
        match self.finishError with
            case Some(e) => Some(e)
            case None =>
                match self.state with
                    case StreamState.Open => None
                    case StreamState.Closing => Some(self.sink.canceledError)
                    case StreamState.Released => Some(self.sink.canceledError)
                    case _ => Some(self.closedError)

    /// The failure every write gives once the stream is finished or closed. There
    /// is no clean answer here, per (2): a write that cannot happen has nothing to
    /// report but why.
    function closedResult(self): Result<Unit, E> =
        match self.unusable() with
            case Some(e) => Error(e)
            case None => Error(self.closedError)

    protected override async function flushPending(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        // Taking the write lock IS the wait for outstanding writes: `write` holds
        // it across its whole start→completion window, so it cannot be granted
        // while any write on this end is still in flight. Once it is granted there
        // is nothing buffered left to push — a `write` only resolves after the
        // host has accepted its bytes — so all that remains is the failure the
        // stream has recorded, which the base reports.
        //
        // It deliberately does NOT poll the result future. That future is
        // one-shot, and reading it while the write end is still open cannot tell
        // "not resolved yet" from "resolved cleanly", so a poll would spend the
        // single read that a real, later error needs. `close` is the definitive
        // check: it ends the stream first, which is what makes the future resolve.
        let _held = use self
            .writeLock
            .acquire()
            .orPanicWith((_: MutexError) => "WasiOutputStream.flush re-entered the write lock")
        ()

    /// Whether this stream could still take a byte, and why not if it could not.
    ///
    /// A finished write stream FAILS here where a finished read stream succeeds:
    /// there is no such thing as a write that legitimately transfers nothing, so
    /// there is no answer for a caller but the reason the stream is gone — the
    /// transport failure if one was recorded, and the origin's "closed" otherwise.
    protected override function checkAlive(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        match self.unusable() with
            case Some(e) => Async.fail(e)
            case None => Async.succeed(())

    /// The failure the stream has already recorded, if any — and, failing that,
    /// the fact that somebody CLOSED this stream, which is a thing that became of
    /// what was written and is not the same as nothing having gone wrong.
    ///
    /// Both states matter and neither is the closer's own. `Closing` is a sibling
    /// close between its cancel and its drop; `Released` is one that finished. A
    /// fiber whose `flush` was queued behind that close is asking what became of
    /// writes the close took away, and `Ok(())` says they were fine. It is the
    /// same answer invariant (9) gives a read that arrives after a close, and for
    /// the same reason: which side of the close the scheduler put you on must not
    /// decide whether you are told.
    ///
    /// `close` mostly does NOT come through here, and that is the difference
    /// between the two questions: it asks what the transport made of the stream,
    /// which a finished close has an answer to whoever asks. This hook is `flush`'s
    /// question — what became of the writes I am waiting on — and a close is a
    /// complete answer to it.
    protected override function recordedFailure(self): Result<Unit, E> =
        match self.endingFailure() with
            case Error(e) => Error(e)
            case Ok(_) =>
                match self.state with
                    case StreamState.Closing => Error(self.sink.canceledError)
                    case StreamState.Released => Error(self.sink.canceledError)
                    case _ => Ok(())

    /// What the TRANSPORT made of this stream, with nothing about who closed it.
    /// The answer `close` reports for the close it performed itself.
    function endingFailure(self): Result<Unit, E> =
        match self.finishError with
            case Some(e) => Error(e)
            case None => Ok(())

    /// End the stream and drop the host handles it owns, silently and without
    /// cancelling anything: drop the write end, read what the host made of it,
    /// then drop the result future. The read comes second because that future
    /// resolves only once the host sees end-of-stream, and is skipped entirely
    /// when a copy already reported the ending, because the runtime consumed the
    /// future there.
    ///
    /// The *finalizer's* close, called by the `Resource` built alongside this
    /// object and by nobody else — which is why it is not `close` and not part of
    /// `AsyncOutputStream`. It needs no cancel: a finalizer runs only after its
    /// scope has unwound every fiber it owns, and interrupting a fiber both
    /// cancels that fiber's copy and gives up its place in the handle's lock
    /// queue, so by the time this runs nothing is posted and nothing is queued.
    /// `close` below has no such guarantee and does the cancelling itself.
    ///
    /// Silent because a finalizer has nowhere to put an error — it latches the
    /// failure into `finishError` and leaves it there, which is what makes an
    /// explicit `close` the only way to see it. Idempotent because `close` drops
    /// these handles early and the Resource then releases over the top.
    public function dropHandles(self): Unit =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Released => ()
            case _ =>
                self.state = StreamState.Released
                // The handles are about to go, so the record's probe must say
                // so before they do — the finalizer path enters here straight
                // from `Open`, without passing `close`.
                self
                    .openFlag
                    .open = false
                // Drop the writable FIRST: the future resolves when the host sees
                // end-of-stream, so reading it before that would spend the single
                // read on "not resolved yet".
                self
                    .sink
                    .dropWritable()
                // `spendEnding` and not `ending`: a copy may already have consulted
                // this future, and reading a spent one-shot future traps. Whether it
                // has is recorded on the handle rather than here, because the fiber
                // that received such a copy's outcome may have been interrupted
                // before it could record anything — see `EndingLedger`. Asking a
                // second time is then free and still yields the reason the first
                // read found, which is what makes an interrupted writer cost this
                // stream nothing.
                match self.sink.spendEnding() with
                    case Error(e) => self.finishError = Some(e)
                    case Ok(_) => ()
                self.sink.dropResult()

    /// Detach every fiber from this stream's writable end, end the stream, and
    /// report what the host made of it. This is the only path on which a late
    /// write failure is observable.
    ///
    /// THREE STEPS, in this order, for every state that still owns the handle —
    /// `Open`, `Ended` and `Torn` alike, since what used to separate them is
    /// recorded elsewhere: whether the result future has been spent is the
    /// handle's `EndingLedger`, not the state.
    ///
    /// LEAVE the current state for `Closing` first, synchronously, before anything
    /// can yield. It is not bookkeeping: it is what a concurrent close sees, and
    /// the `Closing` arm is that close deciding to leave this one alone.
    ///
    /// CANCEL second, covering the queue as well as the operation in flight:
    /// dropping a busy p3 stream traps, and a fiber left queued for a dropped
    /// handle would post its write onto whatever stream the host gives that index
    /// to next. Needed even on a stream that has ended, because a writer that
    /// reached the runtime's per-handle lock queue is still waiting there and
    /// would be granted the lock — and post — the moment the holder lets go.
    ///
    /// DROP last.
    ///
    /// `Released` is the state where none of this may happen: the handle is gone
    /// and its index reused, so a cancel from here would be aimed at whatever
    /// stream the host has since put in its place. `Closing` does nothing at all:
    /// a concurrent close is between its cancel and its drop, and dropping the
    /// handles from here would pull them out from under it. The window is small
    /// and real; making one close wait on another would be a promise not worth its
    /// cost.
    ///
    /// WHAT IS REPORTED is the transport's verdict on the stream — `endingFailure`
    /// — on both arms that have one, and `Ok(())` is a legitimate verdict: a
    /// stream ended cleanly with every byte delivered has nothing to complain
    /// about, however many times it is asked. Idempotent in the ANSWER as well as
    /// the effect, which real callers depend on: a TLS connection closes its
    /// underlying write half from `closeSend` and again when its `Resource` is
    /// released, and a second close that invented a failure would fail the
    /// release.
    ///
    /// `Closing` is the one arm with no verdict to give, and it does not invent
    /// one. Another close is between its cancel and its drop, so it has not
    /// finished reading what the transport made of the stream; what this caller
    /// knows for certain is that its stream was taken away mid-flight, and
    /// `recordedFailure` is where that is written down. `flush` answers from that
    /// hook throughout, because it is asking the other question — what became of
    /// MY writes — and writes a close cancelled were not accepted.
    public override async function close(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        match self.state with
            case StreamState.Closing => await Async.fromResult(self.recordedFailure())
            case StreamState.Released => await Async.fromResult(self.endingFailure())
            case _ =>
                await self.detachAndDrop().uninterruptible()
                await Async.fromResult(self.endingFailure())

    /// The LATCH → CANCEL → DROP sequence, run as ONE uninterruptible unit — the
    /// write twin of `WasiInputStream.detachAndDrop`, and for the same reason.
    ///
    /// Once `state` is latched to `Closing` and the probe flipped, the cancel and
    /// drop MUST both run: an interrupt landing between the latch and the
    /// `CancelStream` node — reachable when the close straddles the fairness
    /// budget's boundary — would discard `detachFibersFrom` (which drains the
    /// writable end's lock queue so a queued writer never posts onto a reused
    /// index) and `dropHandles`, stranding a waiter and leaking the handle.
    /// Masking makes the three atomic; the interrupt is deferred to the mask's
    /// pop, as a bracket release defers one. `endingFailure` stays OUTSIDE the
    /// mask: reporting the verdict is interruptible, only the teardown is not.
    async function detachAndDrop(self): Async<Unit, E> =
        self.state = StreamState.Closing
        // Flip the probe in the same synchronous breath as the state: from here
        // to the drop, a copy-posting step anywhere else must see `stillOpen`
        // false, or it posts on a handle this close is about to take away.
        self
            .openFlag
            .open = false
        await Async<Unit, E>.cancelStreamWrite(self.sink)
        self.dropHandles()

module WasiOutputStream<E> =
    /// A p3 write stream as a `Resource`: acquiring opens it, releasing ends it
    /// and drops the handles it owns. The read twin's rationale applies verbatim —
    /// see `WasiInputStream.resource`.
    ///
    /// `closedError` stays a parameter rather than joining the record: it is not an
    /// ABI fact about the stream end but a policy about what a vanished reader
    /// should be called, and the record deliberately decides nothing.
    ///
    /// The write lock is made inside `acquire`, which is an `Async`, so
    /// `Mutex.make` can be the effect it is.
    ///
    /// `tornWrite` is a PARAMETER and has no default, which is the point: what a
    /// stream owes a half-written buffer depends on what reads the far end, and
    /// only the origin knows that. It was decided here once, for every stream in
    /// the tree, and the decision was wrong for some of them.
    public function resource<E2>(
        closedError: E,
        tornWrite: TornWrite,
        open: () => Async<ComponentStreamWrite<E>, E2>
    ): Resource<AsyncOutputStream<E>, E2>
        where E: IoError =
        let acquire: () => Async<WasiOutputStream<E>, E2> =
            async () =>
                let raw = await open()
                // Flag first, probe over it, record rebuilt, then the stream that
                // flips the flag — see `OpenFlag` for the construction-order knot.
                let openFlag = OpenFlag(true)
                let sink = raw.withStillOpen(() => openFlag.open)
                let writeLock = await Mutex.make()
                let noError: Option<E> = None
                WasiOutputStream<E>(
                    sink,
                    openFlag,
                    closedError,
                    StreamState.Open,
                    noError,
                    tornWrite,
                    false,
                    0i32,
                    writeLock
                )
        let held: Resource<WasiOutputStream<E>, E2> =
            Resource.make(acquire, (s: WasiOutputStream<E>) => Async.thunk(() => s.dropHandles()))
        held.map<AsyncOutputStream<E>>((s: WasiOutputStream<E>) => s)

// --- what a torn write owes, per stream ---------------------------------------
//
// A write of n bytes that stops after k leaves a HOLE, and the hole is invisible:
// there is nothing on the transport to say a message was cut short, so the next
// write is spliced onto the truncated prefix and read as one message. The socket
// test in `standard-io-net` drives the whole of that end to end, interrupt and
// all. What is checked here is the DECISION — which stream owes what — because
// that is what was wrong: it was taken from how long a stream lives, and lifetime
// is not what makes a hole visible.
//
// White-box, reaching straight for `written` and `fenceIncomplete`, because the
// only way to tear a stream honestly is to interrupt a write parked against a
// peer that will not drain, and that needs a peer. Those four members are
// `public` on a class that is itself `internal`, so the reach stops at this
// package — the same latitude `sink` and `dropHandles` already take.
enum FenceProbeError =
    Broken

implement IoError for FenceProbeError

/// A stream end that is never posted to: these tests set `written` by hand.
function fenceProbeSink(): ComponentStreamWrite<FenceProbeError> =
    ComponentStreamWrite<FenceProbeError> {
        writable = Waitable(0i32)
        ending = () => Ok(())
        ledger = EndingLedger.fresh()
        start = (bytes: ReadonlySlice<Uint8>) => panic "the fence probe posts no copies"
        cancel = () => EventPayload(0i32)
        canceledError = FenceProbeError.Broken
        stillOpen = () => true
        dropWritable = () => ()
        dropResult = () => ()
    }

function fenceProbe(policy: TornWrite): WasiOutputStream<FenceProbeError> =
    let noError: Option<FenceProbeError> = None
    // The same wiring `resource` does: flag first, probe over it, record
    // rebuilt, then the stream that flips the flag.
    let openFlag = OpenFlag(true)
    WasiOutputStream<FenceProbeError>(
        fenceProbeSink().withStillOpen(() => openFlag.open),
        openFlag,
        FenceProbeError.Broken,
        StreamState.Open,
        noError,
        policy,
        false,
        0i32,
        Mutex.makeProcessWide()
    )

test "a torn write retires a stream that owes a fence" =
    let stream = fenceProbe(TornWrite.Fence)
    stream.written = 3i32
    stream.fenceIncomplete(5i32)
    // Retired: nothing may be written to it again, and the reason is the one that
    // means "an operation was taken away from you" rather than "the transport is
    // gone" — the transport is fine, which is exactly why nothing else reports it.
    assert stream.unusable().isSome
    assert !stream.owedSeparator

test "a torn write re-frames a stream that cannot be retired" =
    let stream = fenceProbe(TornWrite.Reframe(10u8))
    stream.written = 3i32
    stream.fenceIncomplete(5i32)
    // Still writable, because there is nothing to reopen it with: this is the
    // policy `Console`'s stdout and stderr ask for, and fencing one would let a
    // single interrupted `writeLine` silence the component for good.
    assert stream.unusable().isNone
    // …and the hole is not left unmarked either, which is what "not fencing" used
    // to mean on its own: the next write pays off a separator first, so a
    // line-framed consumer sees a truncated record and a fresh one instead of the
    // two spliced together.
    assert stream.owedSeparator

test "a close is visible to the runtime's posting step through the probe" =
    // The write-side wiring of the check→post fix, asked directly: the record's
    // `stillOpen` — the thing the runtime consults INSIDE the copy-posting step
    // — must go false the moment `close` commits, not when some later state
    // check happens to run. The read side's end-to-end version is the close
    // sweep in `scheduler_window_test`; this pins the wiring the two sides
    // share. White-box like every test here: `sink` is public on an internal
    // class, so the reach stops at this package.
    let stream = fenceProbe(TornWrite.Fence)
    assert stream.sink.stillOpen()
    probeCloseProgram(stream).run()
    assert !stream.sink.stillOpen()

async function probeCloseProgram(stream: WasiOutputStream<FenceProbeError>): Async<Unit, Never> =
    await stream.close().catchAll<Never>((_: FenceProbeError) => Async.succeed(()))

test "a write with nothing out, or everything out, owes nothing either way" =
    // The two edges, and they are the same under both policies. NOTHING out is
    // the ordinary cancelled write: no hole. EVERYTHING out is not a failure as
    // far as the byte stream is concerned — the buffer is whole on the transport —
    // even when the operation itself then failed.
    let untouched = fenceProbe(TornWrite.Reframe(10u8))
    untouched.written = 0i32
    untouched.fenceIncomplete(5i32)
    assert !untouched.owedSeparator
    let complete = fenceProbe(TornWrite.Fence)
    complete.written = 5i32
    complete.fenceIncomplete(5i32)
    assert complete.unusable().isNone
